// Preferences HOW ONPLO LOOKS AND BEHAVES FOR YOU. Every field here follows the PERSON — the same in every
// environment and on every device, so a phone and a desktop agree without anyone setting it
// twice. Nothing here is per environment, and nothing here is any app's.
//
// ALL SIX ARE ALWAYS ANSWERED. A person always has preferences — every one starts at its
// default and is never unset (ONPLO-605) — so an answer missing one is a broken answer, and
// this schema says so rather than letting it validate clean (ONPLO-721).
type Preferences struct {
	// HiddenApps The apps this person has taken off THEIR OWN app grid. HIDING IS COSMETIC AND CHANGES
	// NO ACCESS: the search box still finds a hidden app by name, a link still opens it, and
	// its notifications still arrive in the one stream. It is also theirs alone — a company
	// can never hide an app FOR someone, because the grid is a map of the platform and not
	// of entitlements.
	//
	// AN EMPTY LIST IS THE WHOLE GRID, which is what "Show everything" restores. An id naming
	// no Onplo app comes back as a 422 naming the field.
	//
	// Examples: ["finance","projects"]
	HiddenApps []string `json:"hidden_apps"`

	// Language A BCP 47 tag, and THIS is where it is written — it is read-only on the profile so that
	// one preference has exactly one writable home.
	//
	// BOUNDED, BECAUSE AN UNBOUNDED STRING IN A BODY MAKES THE BODY LIMIT UNPROVABLE — the
	// body walker in `body.test.ts` fills every string to its length and proves the largest
	// valid body still fits. 35 is the longest tag RFC 5646's grammar produces in practice.
	//
	// THE PATTERN IS THE COLUMN'S, CHARACTER FOR CHARACTER (`account.bcp47_tag`, migration
	// 0002): a first subtag of two to eight letters, then subtags of one to eight letters or
	// digits. So the door refuses exactly what the column refuses, and no tag it accepts can
	// come back as anything but stored. It holds the tag's GRAMMAR and not the REGISTRY, which
	// moves several times a year while a contract does not, so `zz` is a well-formed tag and
	// is accepted.
	//
	// IT WAS LOOSER TWICE, and each time for the same reason. Until 2026-09-30 it was a bare
	// character class, and `a`, `1ab`, `x-private` and `en--US` passed the door and failed the
	// column. Then a `not` refused those and still let a subtag longer than eight characters
	// through to the service (ONPLO-721), because the body walker could fill only a run of
	// one letter. The walker now fills a field from its own `examples` (ONPLO-789), which is
	// why the example below is load-bearing: remove it and the body-limit proof has nothing
	// to build this field from.
	//
	// Examples: nl-BE
	Language string `json:"language"`

	// ShowAppNames The names under the tiles on the app grid. ON BY DEFAULT — turning it off is the
	// cinematic look, for people who know their icons.
	//
	// Examples: true
	ShowAppNames bool `json:"show_app_names"`

	// Theme `system` IS THE DEFAULT, and the reason is stated rather than assumed: an app that
	// ignores the choice a person already made on their own device is louder than it needs
	// to be.
	//
	// Examples: system
	Theme PreferencesTheme `json:"theme"`

	// TimeZone An IANA zone. The clock times are shown in FOR YOU: it changes what you SEE and never
	// what a flow DOES — schedules and deadlines are counted in the environment's zone, so
	// moving country never quietly moves your company's 09:00.
	//
	// THE PATTERN IS THE DATABASE'S, DELIBERATELY. `account.iana_zone` refuses anything
	// outside this character set, and a bare `type: string` here let a request through the
	// door that the column then refused with 23514 — which the interceptor can only render
	// as a 500. A wrong time zone is the caller's mistake and must come back as a 422 naming
	// the field, so the door has to be at least as strict as the column behind it.
	//
	// THE PATTERN REFUSES NONSENSE, AND THE SERVICE REFUSES THE REST. The real list moves
	// several times a year and lives in the runtime's own zone database, not in a contract,
	// so `Mars/Olympus` passes this pattern — and the service then asks that database and
	// answers a zone no clock knows, or a bare offset such as `+01`, with `PATCH
	// /me/preferences`' 422 at `/body/time_zone` (ONPLO-527).
	//
	// IT MOVED HERE FROM THE PROFILE on 2026-09-30 (ONPLO-604), where it had been writable
	// beside the facts other people see. `Profile.time_zone` still answers it, read-only, as
	// `Profile.language` answers the field above.
	//
	// Examples: Europe/Brussels
	TimeZone string `json:"time_zone"`

	// UnreadNotificationsOnApps What an app's tile shows when it has unread notifications. `number` IS THE DEFAULT,
	// because the notification stream already counts per app and a number is what a person
	// acts on.
	//
	// Examples: number
	UnreadNotificationsOnApps PreferencesUnreadNotificationsOnApps `json:"unread_notifications_on_apps"`
}

// PreferencesTheme `system` IS THE DEFAULT, and the reason is stated rather than assumed: an app that
// ignores the choice a person already made on their own device is louder than it needs
// to be.
//
// Examples: system
type PreferencesTheme string

// PreferencesUnreadNotificationsOnApps What an app's tile shows when it has unread notifications. `number` IS THE DEFAULT,
