// ClaimCompanyDomainWithBodyWithResponse Claim a domain
//
// Says a domain name is this company's, and answers the record to publish so Onplo can see it
// is. The domain itself — `example.com`, never a page or a mailbox on it. It is Pending until
// the record answers; nothing that depends on a verified domain works on a Pending one.
//
// The same name is never claimed twice inside one company, compared without regard to case.
// Another company may claim the same name: a claim proves nothing, and only the one that can
// publish the record becomes Verified.
//
// A name longer than 233 characters is a 422 naming `name`: the record's host name would pass
// the 253 characters a host name may be, so nobody could publish it.
//
// A name that can never be public is a 422 naming `name` too: one ending in `.internal`,
// `.local`, `.localhost`, `.test` or `.invalid`. Nobody outside a private network can see a
// record published there, so the claim could never be proved, and Onplo never asks about such
// a name. An address written as a name, `192.0.2.10`, is no domain name and is refused as any
// malformed name is.
//
// One of Onplo's own domain names, or any name under one, is a 422 naming `name` as well: no
// company may claim a name Onplo itself serves under.
//
// ONPLO DOES NOT LOOK YET. Until the check is built a claim stays Pending whatever the company
// publishes, and nothing is switched on by a claim.
//
