// ConfirmEmailAddressWithBodyWithResponse Confirm an email address, from the link Onplo mailed to it
//
// THE ONE OPERATION HERE THAT TAKES NO CREDENTIAL (decision 0623, Jonas, 2026-09-29). The link
// Onplo mails to an added address confirms it on one click, SIGNED IN OR NOT: its holder can
// read that inbox, and that is all the click has to prove. Sending a person to sign in first
// would add a step on every phone whose mail app opens a browser they are not signed in to, for
// no proof the inbox did not already give.
//
// IT CONFIRMS THE ADDRESS, AND ONE CASE DOES ONE THING MORE. A person with no address marked has
// the address they confirm made the one Onplo writes to (decision 0935, amending decision 0623).
// Every other address only confirms: moving that marker stays a deliberate `PATCH` by the person,
// signed in. It signs nobody in. It opens nothing behind it. Email is never a credential (decision
// 0557), and this operation is why that sentence can stay true: no credential goes in, and none
// comes out.
//
// IT NAMES THE ADDRESS AND NOTHING ABOUT THE ACCOUNT. Whoever holds the mail may open the link —
// a shared inbox, a forwarded message — so the answer carries the address it confirmed and never
// the name, the username or any id of the account that added it.
//
// THE TOKEN TRAVELS IN THE BODY, never in the path or a query string: a URL is written to logs
// a body is not. The page the link lands on reads it and sends it here.
//
// NO 401, NO 403 AND NO 503: it never asks who is calling, so it can refuse nobody for who they
// are, and the service that would answer that question is never reached. An `Authorization`
// header sent anyway is not read. Being signed in changes one line of text on the page and
// nothing about this call.
//
// IT IS LIMITED BY HOW OFTEN IT IS CALLED, NEVER BY WHO CALLS (decision 0732). With no
// credential there is nobody to charge a call to, so the server counts calls a minute from one
// address and from everyone together, and answers 429 past either, with the seconds to wait.
// A person clicks a link once or twice; the limit is far above that and is not stated here,
// because it is a setting of the server and `Retry-After` tells a client all it needs.
//
// Takes any type of body and a specified content type, and returns a wrapper object for the known response body format(s).
//
// Corresponds with POST /email-confirmations (the `ConfirmEmailAddress` operationId).
func (c *ClientWithResponses) ConfirmEmailAddressWithBodyWithResponse(ctx context.Context, contentType string, body io.Reader, reqEditors ...RequestEditorFn) (*ConfirmEmailAddressResponse, error) {
	rsp, err := c.ConfirmEmailAddressWithBody(ctx, contentType, body, reqEditors...)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	return ParseConfirmEmailAddressResponse(rsp)
}

