// ClearProfilePhotoWithResponse Take your profile picture down
//
// The file goes the whole-object lifecycle way System Storage uses — handed to the owning app
// first, then Trash — and `photo` answers `null` afterwards. Every address already issued for
// it stops resolving at once.
//
// NOT ALL OF THAT IS TRUE YET, AND IT IS SAID HERE (ONPLO-686). Storage's `OwnedFilesService` offers
// the owning app no delete, so the file stays at System / Account / Profile, counted in your storage,
// and an address already issued lives out its minutes. What holds from the first moment: `photo`
// answers `null`, and no new address is issued for the picture. The rest waits on Storage giving
// Account that delete.
//
// IT IS ITS OWN OPERATION RATHER THAN AN EMPTY WRITE, because clearing is a delete: a put with
// no bytes would be a second spelling of the same act, and a reader would have to know which
// one this file meant.
